# Incident Ticket — INC-003

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Ticket ID** | INC-003 |
| **Title** | Rogue DHCP server on an access port — DHCP snooping protection validation |
| **Reported by** | Network Engineer (deliberate fault injection — recreating the original Sales VLAN incident) |
| **Affected segment** | Sales VLAN (VLAN 10), Switch0 access layer |
| **Severity** | High (simulated) — this is a direct re-creation of the incident that originally motivated this redesign |
| **Status** | Resolved — DHCP snooping confirmed blocking unauthorized DHCP servers |

---

## Summary

A rogue router, deliberately configured to act as an unauthorized DHCP server, was connected to an **unused, untrusted access port** on Switch0 — recreating the exact real-world incident that started this redesign (*"an employee accidentally plugged in a personal wireless router that handed out its own DHCP leases, breaking connectivity for a dozen users on the Sales VLAN"*). A legitimate client on the same VLAN, on a different port, then repeatedly released and renewed its DHCP lease to confirm it could never receive an address from the rogue device.

---

## Setup

- **Rogue-Device** (Router-PT) connected to an unused access port on Switch0 (Sales VLAN 10), with a deliberately incorrect DHCP pool:
  ```
  ip dhcp pool ROGUE-SALES
   network 10.10.10.0 255.255.255.0
   default-router 10.10.10.99
   dns-server 1.1.1.1
  ```
  (Default gateway and DNS intentionally set to values that do **not** match the legitimate configuration, so any successful rogue lease would be immediately obvious.)
- **Legitimate DHCP server** remains Core A, serving the real `SALES` pool with `default-router 10.10.10.2` and `dns-server 8.8.8.8`.
- **Test client:** PC5, on a separate port on the same switch/VLAN.

---

## Diagnostic Steps & Evidence

### 1. Confirm DHCP snooping baseline on Switch0

```
Switch(config)#do sh ip dhcp snooping
Switch DHCP snooping is enabled
DHCP snooping is configured on following VLANs:
10,20,30
Insertion of option 82 is enabled
Option 82 on untrusted port is not allowed
Verification of hwaddr field is enabled

Interface              Trusted   Rate limit (pps)
---------------------- -------   ----------------
FastEthernet0/1        yes       unlimited
FastEthernet0/2        yes       unlimited
FastEthernet0/3        no        unlimited
FastEthernet0/4        no        unlimited
FastEthernet0/5        yes       unlimited
```
Only the infrastructure-facing ports (Fa0/1, 0/2, 0/5 — uplinks to cores/peer switches) are trusted. PC-facing ports (Fa0/3, 0/4) are untrusted, as required — this is exactly where the rogue device and legitimate clients connect.

### 2. Confirm the legitimate client's lease before the rogue device is active

```
C:\>ipconfig
IPv4 Address...: 10.10.10.6
Default Gateway...: 10.10.10.2

C:\>ipconfig /release
C:\>ipconfig /renew
IP Address...: 10.10.10.6
Default Gateway...: 10.10.10.2
DNS Server...: 8.8.8.8
```
Baseline confirmed: correct gateway and DNS from the legitimate server.

### 3. Connect the rogue device to an untrusted access port and repeat lease renewal

With the rogue device active on the same VLAN:
```
C:\>ipconfig /release
   IP Address...: 0.0.0.0
   Default Gateway...: 0.0.0.0

C:\>ipconfig /renew
   IP Address...: 10.10.10.6
   Default Gateway...: 10.10.10.2
   DNS Server...: 8.8.8.8

C:\>ipconfig /release
C:\>ipconfig /renew
   IP Address...: 10.10.10.8
   Default Gateway...: 10.10.10.2
   DNS Server...: 8.8.8.8
```
Across multiple release/renew cycles — the assigned IP address itself varies between attempts (`.6`, then `.8`, normal pool allocation behavior) — but the **gateway and DNS server are correct and unchanged every single time**. At no point did the client receive the rogue device's values (`10.10.10.99` / `1.1.1.1`).

### 4. Confirm via the DHCP snooping binding table that only legitimate leases were ever tracked

```
Switch(config-if)#do sh ip dhcp snooping binding
MacAddress          IpAddress       Lease(sec)  Type           VLAN  Interface
------------------  --------------  ----------  -------------  ----  --------------------
00:60:3E:81:0D:01   10.10.10.7      0           dhcp-snooping  10    FastEthernet0/3
00:60:2F:D1:0B:4E    10.10.10.8     0           dhcp-snooping  10    FastEthernet0/4
Total number of bindings: 2
```
Only two bindings exist, both tracked as legitimate `dhcp-snooping` entries tied to known client MAC addresses on known ports — nothing from the rogue device appears anywhere in the table, confirming its offers never completed a lease with any client.

