## Root Cause (of the simulated scenario)

Not applicable — this was a controlled, deliberate re-creation of the original incident to validate the fix, not an unplanned fault.

---

## Resolution

No remediation was required. DHCP snooping, correctly scoped to VLANs 10/20/30 with only genuine infrastructure ports marked trusted, silently dropped the rogue device's offers at the switch before they could ever reach a client — exactly as designed. The legitimate client retained a correct, working lease throughout every test cycle, even with an active rogue DHCP server present on the same broadcast domain.

---

## Prevention / Design Notes

- This test directly validates the fix for the original incident that motivated this entire redesign: *"only the legitimate DHCP server may hand out leases; anything else plugged into an access port must be blocked automatically."*
- Unlike port security (which protects based on *which device* is connected), DHCP snooping protects the *entire VLAN*, regardless of which specific port the rogue device or the victim client are on — confirmed here by testing the rogue device and the legitimate client on two different ports.
- **Recommendation:** periodically audit `show ip dhcp snooping` trusted-interface lists after any physical re-cabling or topology change — an infrastructure port accidentally left untrusted (or a new rogue-adjacent port accidentally marked trusted) would silently reopen this exact vulnerability. This was in fact discovered as a real gap during initial DHCP snooping configuration on this network (a peer-switch trunk port was initially missed from the trusted list), underscoring that this check should be routine, not one-time.
